Reviewers generally agree that the paper presents a technically sound and clearly written theoretical result, showing the existence of statistically undetectable backdoors in a restricted class of neural networks, even under white-box access. The cryptographic framing, proofs, and formal guarantees were viewed as strong.

However, several concerns limited enthusiasm:
(1) the result relies on strong architectural assumptions, with most of the technical burden concentrated in the first compressing layer, raising questions about generality and realism;
(2) the proposed invariance-based “collision” backdoor departs from standard backdoor notions, and some reviewers questioned its practical security relevance, suggesting it may be closer to watermarking or provenance;
(3) the incremental novelty over prior work on JL embeddings and collision hardness was debated;
(4) practical deployment and threat-model assumptions remain unclear.

The rebuttal clarifies motivation and positioning and partially addresses these points, but concerns about practical impact and scope largely remain
